WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.954
Tailspin Toys owns the toy-line designs and manufacturing instructions: its intellectual property.

00:06.274 --> 00:11.530
Contoso is the subcontractor, operating the robots, printers, and infrastructure.

00:11.850 --> 00:14.386
Tailspin sends encrypted instructions.

00:14.706 --> 00:20.149
Encryption in use protects them in confidential-container memory while approved code processes them.

00:20.469 --> 00:22.501
Attestation checks the runtime.

00:22.821 --> 00:29.762
Secure key release makes Tailspin's keys available only to the approved identity and exact measured
container policy.

00:30.082 --> 00:35.945
Contoso engineers operate the infrastructure, but cannot open a shell into this protected workload.

00:36.265 --> 00:38.954
Now Tailspin revokes command-key access.

00:39.274 --> 00:40.769
The key is disabled.

00:41.089 --> 00:42.744
Further key release fails.

00:43.064 --> 00:48.018
When the app detects this, plaintext commands disappear and the fictional line locks.

00:48.338 --> 00:51.147
Ciphertext and status remain available.

00:51.467 --> 00:55.119
Tailspin owns the IP and controls permission to process it.
